    "created_at": "2016-02-03T18:29:26Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-02-03T18:29:26Z",
    "body": "> @domenic the intent of a 3xx is to redirect. The current implementation does not satisfy this intent, as if it were a true redirect, it would not result in duplicate execution, it would result in a module registry entry at the redirected location.\n\nI disagree with this. People are referring to the module by two different names---who can say what their intent was? Should we also normalize `import './foo.js#x'` and `import './foo.js#y'` to the same? What about `?x` and `?y`? What about `foo.js` and `bar.js` where those two files have the same contents? What about server-side symlinks?\n\nI don't think a redirect implies that they should be the same registry entry (or a proxy module delegating to another registry entry), in any sense. It just implies the semantics of a HTTP redirect. One of the important parts of those semantics is that request URL and response URL differ, which can definitely be done intentionally.\n\n> I'm not asking to change the platform semantics, I'm just asking if there's ways to make it behave as a true redirect, and if not why it is implemented at all, as it seems like a footgun.\n\nI disagree that it's a footgun. Referring to a module by two different URLs seems like a pretty good reason to execute it twice.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
