    "created_at": "2016-02-03T18:43:38Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-02-03T18:43:38Z",
    "body": "@domenic to clarify the redirect intent, lets consider a good old-fashioned redirect scenario - `https://modules.com` is a module server. As a user of this module server, on my own custom website I write my modules like:\n\n``` html\n<script type=\"module\">\nimport $ from 'https://modules.com/jquery.js';\nimport _ from 'https://modules.com/lodash.js';\n</script>\n```\n\nFor argument's sake, lets imagine the jQuery of the future depends on lodash, so it contains `import _ from './lodash.js'` within its source.\n\nNow `https://modules.com` decides to move its domain to the much better sounding name, `https://awesome-modules.com`.\n\nThey set up 301 redirects for all the modules, but now my website which uses the old URLs to `https://modules.com` is executing lodash twice. Thus `modules.com` cannot use 301's to effectively redirect modules to the new domain.\n\nIn this very traditional sense of a redirect, this is what I mean by it not fulfilling the intent.\n\nI wanted to suggest the shell module approach which does satisfy the intent, but I completely understand if there is no interest or it is unsuitable here. But please be aware that work is being made for the loader spec to support this, without a clear benefit, which could be avoided as well as satisfying the use case through the shell module approach mentioned.\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
